## Product Cluster C: Gastroenterology (Acid-Related Disorders)

### Napizole (Omeprazole 20 mg)

Mechanism:
- Irreversible inhibition of the gastric H+/K+-ATPase (proton pump), suppressing basal and stimulated acid secretion; onset within hours with maximal effect after repeated once-daily dosing; duration of action up to 36 hours.[^16][^17]

Indications:
- GERD, erosive esophagitis, duodenal and gastric ulcers, H. pylori eradication regimens (as part of combination therapy), and pathological hypersecretory conditions. Maintenance therapy may be required for recurrent erosive esophagitis.[^16][^18][^19]

Dosing:
- Common adult starting dose is 20–40 mg once daily for 4–8 weeks (GERD/ulcer indications), with possible extension or maintenance at the lowest effective dose. Pediatric dosing is weight-based and indication-specific. Administer 30–60 minutes before a meal (preferably breakfast).[^16][^18][^19]

Safety:
- Common adverse effects: headache, GI upset, diarrhea, nausea. Long-term therapy requires vigilance for hypomagnesemia, B12 deficiency, and enteric infections. Interactions involve CYP2C19 and CYP3A4; PPIs may alter absorption of drugs dependent on gastric pH. Use lowest effective dose and duration consistent with clinical response.[^16][^17][^18][^19]

Contraindications:
- Hypersensitivity to omeprazole or substituted benzimidazoles; avoid concurrent nelfinavir. Consider hepatic impairment dose reductions where appropriate.[^19]

Table 7 consolidates dosing by indication.

Table 7. Omeprazole dosing and administration

| Indication | Adult dose | Duration | Administration |
|---|---|---|---|
| GERD/erosive esophagitis | 20–40 mg once daily | 4–8 weeks; may extend | 30–60 min before breakfast[^16][^18] |
| Duodenal ulcer | 20 mg once daily | 4–8 weeks | As above[^19] |
| Gastric ulcer | 40 mg once daily | 4–8 weeks | As above[^19] |
| H. pylori eradication | 20 mg twice daily | Part of triple/quadruple therapy | With antibiotics per regimen[^19] |
| Maintenance | Lowest effective dose | Individualized | Periodic review[^16][^18] |

Interpretation: Omeprazole’s robust acid suppression anchors many acid-related treatment pathways; safe long-term use hinges on dose optimization and monitoring for metabolic and infectious risks.

