## Product Cluster E: Neurology (GABA Analog)

### Neurontin (Gabapentin 300 mg)

Class and mechanism:
- Gabapentin is a GABA analog whose analgesic and anticonvulsant effects are primarily mediated via binding to the alpha2-delta subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels, reducing excitatory neurotransmitter release. It does not convert to GABA directly.[^21]

Indications:
- Adjunctive therapy for focal seizures and management of postherpetic neuralgia; commonly used off-label for other neuropathic pain conditions per clinical practice.[^21]

Dosing:
- Titrate per clinical indication and tolerability. Typical adult regimens range from 300 mg once on day 1, to 300 mg twice daily on day 2, and 300 mg three times daily on day 3, then further titration to 300–600 mg three times daily (range often 900–3600 mg/day) depending on response and renal function. Dosing adjustments are required in renal impairment.[^21]

Safety:
- Dizziness, somnolence, ataxia, peripheral edema, and visual disturbances (e.g., blurred vision) are among the more common adverse effects. Caution when operating machinery or driving, particularly during dose escalation. Geriatric patients may be more sensitive to CNS effects; gradual titration mitigates falls and confusion risks.[^21]

Interpretation: Gabapentin’s flexible dosing and broad neuropathic applications make it a staple in neurology and pain management; renal dosing is central to safe use, especially in older adults and those with comorbidities.

Table 9. Gabapentin dosing by indication and renal status (adults)

| Indication | Initial titration | Typical maintenance | Renal adjustment |
|---|---|---|---|
| Seizures (adjunct) | 300 mg day 1; 300 mg bid day 2; 300 mg tid day 3 | 300–600 mg tid (900–1800 mg/day); up to 3600 mg/day | Reduce per CrCl; consult renal dosing table[^21] |
| Neuropathic pain | Similar titration | 300–600 mg tid | Reduce per CrCl; monitor sedation[^21] |
| Geriatrics | Slower titration | Lower initial maintenance | Increased fall/CNS risk; monitor closely[^21] |

Interpretation: Titration schedules aim to balance therapeutic gains with tolerability; renal function dictates the ceiling for safe dosing.

