## Safety, Contraindications, and Drug Interactions: Cross-Product Matrix

To support clinical decision-making, Table 10 synthesizes key interactions and precautions across the cluster. It emphasizes monitoring and avoidance strategies that should be applied irrespective of brand.

Table 10. Cross-Product Drug Interaction Matrix

| Product | Key interacting class | Mechanism | Action |
|---|---|---|---|
| Ivabradine | CYP3A4 inhibitors/inducers; HR-lowering drugs (e.g., digoxin, beta-blockers, amiodarone) | Increased exposure and bradycardia risk; additive HR lowering | Avoid potent inhibitors/inducers; titrate HR and ECG monitoring[^10][^12] |
| Ivabradine | QT-prolonging agents | Additive QT effects | Monitor QT; correct electrolytes[^10][^12] |
| Indapamide | Other diuretics; QT-prolonging drugs; lithium | Electrolyte loss and lithium toxicity | Monitor K+, Mg2+; adjust lithium dose[^13][^14] |
| Omeprazole | CYP2C19 substrates/inducers/inhibitors; nelfinavir | Altered metabolism; loss of nelfinavir exposure | Avoid nelfinavir; review CYP2C19 genotype effects; dose adjust[^16][^19] |
| Fluticasone (intranasal) | CYP3A4 inhibitors | Increased systemic steroid exposure | Use lowest effective dose; monitor for adrenal effects[^2][^4] |
| Mometasone (intranasal) | CYP3A4 inhibitors | Similar steroid exposure considerations | As above; technique review and periodic reassessment[^8][^9] |
| Sildenafil (to confirm) | Nitrates; alpha-blockers; antihypertensives; CYP3A4 inhibitors | Severe hypotension; additive BP lowering; increased exposure | Avoid nitrates; titrate alpha-blockers; review antihypertensives; adjust dose with strong inhibitors[^22] |
| Nitazoxanide | Limited major interactions | — | Confirm with label; monitor in hepatic/renal impairment[^20] |

Interpretation: The matrix highlights where physiologic targets overlap (HR, QT, CYP3A4, pH), enabling proactive monitoring and avoidance strategies at the point of prescribing.

